          "essay": "Luke records that certain men from Judea taught the brethren: “Unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved.” Paul and Barnabas disputed with them; the matter was referred to Jerusalem. After much debate, Peter recalled how God had given the Holy Spirit to the Gentiles; Paul and Barnabas reported signs among the nations; James proposed a judgment grounded in the prophets. The assembly then wrote: “It has seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us…”—the classic formula of conciliar authority. The decree is not a private opinion; it is the decision of the Church’s first council, received as binding. Every later ecumenical council inherits this pattern: question, gathering, testimony, judgment, and a letter that binds the churches. The text of Acts 15 is therefore not mere background narrative; it is the primary document of the apostolic magisterium on the entrance of the nations into the covenant people without the yoke of the full Mosaic ceremonial law.",
