            "The path of intervention is itself part of the doctrine of the magisterium. Irenaeus, Tertullian, and other teachers expose Gnostic myths and appeal to succession from the Apostles and the common creed of the churches. Bishops refuse communion to teachers who deny the Creator or the flesh of Christ. The act that closed — or sought to close — the question may be stated in these terms: No single ecumenical definition in this century; the rule of faith, the emerging New Testament canon, and episcopal succession function as the Church’s public criteria against private revelation and myth. Such an act does not claim to invent revelation. It claims to judge a present dispute by the revelation already given and already lived in the churches. It may take the form of a synodal letter, a conciliar definition, a papal decree, or a disciplinary canon with doctrinal bite. Reception across the churches is the ordinary test of whether the act is recognized as the Church’s own. Resistance, delay, and even schism can follow; they do not by themselves prove the judgment false, any more than acceptance by a majority in one generation proves it true. The Catholic claim rests on the continuity of the apostolic faith and the authority Christ gave to the college of the apostles with Peter at their head.",
