            "The long memory of Montanism is itself part of the Church’s teaching office: she does not only define; she remembers what she has defined, and she refuses to treat a closed question as if it were still open. The rejected positions remain valuable as warnings: they show how the language of Scripture and of ascetic zeal can be retained while the substance of the faith is denied. The intervention remains a model of how the Church decides under pressure. The result remains a fact of communion or of separation that later ages must still interpret. To read Montanism carefully is therefore to practice a Catholic habit of mind: to ask what was defined, what was only disciplined, what was received, and what still obliges. It is also to see that doctrine develops in clarity without dissolving into contradiction — that the Church can speak more explicitly in a later century without disowning what she confessed in an earlier one. That is why this controversy, and not only the abstract theory of infallibility, belongs on any serious map of the magisterium.",
