          "essay": "Eusebius reports that a significant difference existed between the churches of Asia, who kept the Pascha on the fourteenth of Nisan regardless of the day of the week, and the churches that kept the feast only on the Lord’s Day following. Synods were held in Palestine, Rome, Pontus, Gaul, and elsewhere; the Asian churches, represented by figures such as Polycrates of Ephesus, defended their apostolic custom. Victor of Rome, according to the same account, attempted to cut off the dioceses of Asia from the common unity over the question—an act that already treats Roman judgment as bearing on the communion of distant churches. Irenaeus of Lyons wrote to Victor urging that the peace of the Church not be broken over a custom that earlier Roman bishops had tolerated in diversity of practice while agreeing in faith. The episode is therefore double: it witnesses to a strong Roman claim and to a pacific restraint urged by a great bishop of Gaul.\n\nBecause Victor’s autograph letters do not survive in the Catholic Library or the major corpora, the primary source given below is Eusebius’s Church History, Book V, chapters 23–25 (New Advent / Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers series)—the standard historical record of the papal act, the Asian defense, and Irenaeus’s mediation.",
