          "essay": "Marcion of Sinope, active in the mid-second century, taught a radical separation between the God of the Old Testament and the Father of Jesus Christ, and assembled a truncated scripture of a mutilated Luke and selected Pauline letters. At Rome he was refused communion. The act is magisterial in the proper sense: the Church of the Apostles will not receive a gospel that discards the Creator and the Scriptures of Israel. Irenaeus, Tertullian, and later writers preserve the shape of the controversy; the Roman refusal itself is the local church’s exercise of the power to bind and loose in matters of the faith’s identity. The “rule of faith”—the baptismal confession of Father, Son, and Spirit, of the one God who made heaven and earth, of the Incarnation, Passion, and Resurrection—becomes the explicit criterion against secret systems. In the 100s, therefore, the magisterium is already defining the Church not only by who is ordained but by what may not be denied without leaving the communion of the faithful.",
