            "To understand Donatism is to understand how the Church distinguishes the deposit of faith from a plausible but fatal innovation. The dispute turned on this: Does the validity of the sacraments and the identity of the true Church depend on the personal holiness of the minister and on a pure line free from traditores? The setting was the 4th, with its inherited Scriptures, liturgical practice, and bonds of communion — and also with its unresolved tensions. The controversy known as Donatism forced the Church to answer a question that could not remain open without wounding the faith or the communion of the faithful. Does the validity of the sacraments and the identity of the true Church depend on the personal holiness of the minister and on a pure line free from traditores? That question did not arise  What was at stake was not a preference among theological styles. If the Church received the wrong answer as her own, she would either bind consciences where Christ left them free, or leave them free where the faith requires obedience; she would either confess less of Christ than the apostles handed down, or add to the gospel a condition the apostles did not impose. Controversies of this order therefore compel a public act: a judgment that can be recognized as the voice of the same Church that baptizes, absolves, and offers the Eucharist.",
