            "Consequences followed at once in preaching, in the catechumenate, and in the boundaries of communion. The Western Church binds itself to the necessity of grace without erasing free will. What had been argued in synods became, for those who received the judgment, a norm for the altar and the pulpit. The later career of the controversy is not a footnote; it is the proof that the Church continued to mean what she had said. Orange (529), the Scholastic synthesis, Trent on justification, and later controversies over Jansenism all stand downstream of this fifth-century struggle. Later councils and popes returned to the same principles when the old error reappeared under new names. The dossier of Pelagianism — its canons, letters, and acts — entered the ordinary tools of Catholic theology. Theologians cite it, distinguish its doctrinal core from its contingent discipline, and apply it whenever a fresh dispute threatens to reopen a closed question. In that way the magisterium is not only an event in the past but a living memory in the present.",
            "The long memory of Pelagianism is itself part of the Church’s teaching office: she does not only define; she remembers what she has defined, and she refuses to treat a closed question as if it were still open. The rejected positions remain valuable as warnings: they show how the language of Scripture and of ascetic zeal can be retained while the substance of the faith is denied. The intervention remains a model of how the Church decides under pressure. The result remains a fact of communion or of separation that later ages must still interpret. To read Pelagianism carefully is therefore to practice a Catholic habit of mind: to ask what was defined, what was only disciplined, what was received, and what still obliges. It is also to see that doctrine develops in clarity without dissolving into contradiction — that the Church can speak more explicitly in a later century without disowning what she confessed in an earlier one. That is why this controversy, and not only the abstract theory of infallibility, belongs on any serious map of the magisterium.",
            "In the end, Pelagianism teaches the same lesson every major controversy teaches when it is read from within the Catholic claim: the faith is public, the Church is visible, and her judgments are meant to be received. Private certainty, imperial convenience, and the pure remnant that separates from the apostolic see have all, at different times, offered themselves as safer paths. The Catholic path remains the harder one of communion under a judgment that can be named, dated, and handed on. Those who study the question, the positions, the intervention, and the act together are not merely learning a chapter of religious history. They are learning how the Church knows what she knows — and how she refuses to forget it."
