          "essay": "The Carolingian Controversy is the foundation stone of Western Eucharistic theology as a contested science. Paschasius writes to assert that the faithful do not receive a mere sign: the altar holds the true body of Christ. Ratramnus writes to protect the distinction between the sacramental order and a crude capharnaite chewing of flesh, stressing figure and spiritual effect. Neither man denies that the Eucharist is sacred and efficacious; they diverge on the mode of identity between the historical body and the sacramental body. The ninth-century Magisterium does not yet speak with Lateran IV’s precision. What it leaves open, the medieval Church will later bind: the presence is real and substantial, not a bare symbol—while still not a local, carnal presence in the manner of ordinary bodies. For this archive the controversy belongs beside the Eucharist concept and the theological tree that runs from Manna and the Paschal Lamb through the Carolingian dispute to transubstantiation."
