            "Catholic teaching is clarified under fire. Conciliarism is a case study of that clarification. At the center stood a single, unavoidable question: Is a general council superior to the pope, so that it may judge, limit, or depose him as an ordinary constitutional rule? The setting was the 15th, with its inherited Scriptures, liturgical practice, and bonds of communion — and also with its unresolved tensions. The controversy known as Conciliarism forced the Church to answer a question that could not remain open without wounding the faith or the communion of the faithful. Is a general council superior to the pope, so that it may judge, limit, or depose him as an ordinary constitutional rule? That question did not arise in the abstract. It arose from prea What was at stake was not a preference among theological styles. If the Church received the wrong answer as her own, she would either bind consciences where Christ left them free, or leave them free where the faith requires obedience; she would either confess less of Christ than the apostles handed down, or add to the gospel a condition the apostles did not impose. Controversies of this order therefore compel a public act: a judgment that can be recognized as the voice of the same Church that baptizes, absolves, and offers the Eucharist.",
            "The parties did not speak past each other in mere slogans; they offered coherent accounts of Scripture and practice. One side held that the council represents the Church and stands above the personal holder of the Roman see in certain conditions—or even as a rule. The opposing claim was that the pope, as successor of Peter, has primacy of jurisdiction that no council may turn into a standing superiority over him; Constance’s emergency acts are not a new constitution of the Church. Each position could appeal to texts, to local custom, or to the fear of scandal. Each could present itself as the guardian of holiness or of unity. The Catholic resolution had to do more than prefer one rhetoric to another. It had to name, as far as the dispute required, what belongs to the deposit of faith, what belongs to legitimate discipline, and what must be rejected as incompatible with the rule of faith. Where the controversy touched the sacraments, the Church had to protect both the objectivity of the rite and the necessity of communion with the body that Christ founded. Where it touched the Trinity or the Incarnation, she had to protect the object of worship itself. Where it touched jurisdiction and primacy, she had to say who may bind and loose for the whole Church and on what grounds a particular church must yield.",
            "The path of intervention is itself part of the doctrine of the magisterium. Constance; Basel; papal restoration under Eugene IV and successors; later rejection of conciliarist dogma. The act that closed — or sought to close — the question may be stated in these terms: Florence’s strong primacy teaching; eventual papal and theological refusal of Haec Sancta as a dogmatic norm; Vatican I’s later definitions presuppose this refusal. Such an act does not claim to invent revelation. It claims to judge a present dispute by the revelation already given and already lived in the churches. It may take the form of a synodal letter, a conciliar definition, a papal decree, or a disciplinary canon with doctrinal bite. Reception across the churches is the ordinary test of whether the act is recognized as the Church’s own. Resistance, delay, and even schism can follow; they do not by themselves prove the judgment false, any more than acceptance by a majority in one generation proves it true. The Catholic claim rests on the continuity of the apostolic faith and the authority Christ gave to the college of the apostles with Peter at their head.",
            "Consequences followed at once in preaching, in the catechumenate, and in the boundaries of communion. Conciliarism declines as a constitutional theory of the Catholic Church, though reform by council remains a recurring hope. What had been argued in synods became, for those who received the judgment, a norm for the altar and the pulpit. The later career of the controversy is not a footnote; it is the proof that the Church continued to mean what she had said. Vatican I (1870) defines primacy and infallibility in a way incompatible with classical conciliarist superiority. Later councils and popes returned to the same principles when the old error reappeared under new names. The dossier of Conciliarism — its canons, letters, and acts — entered the ordinary tools of Catholic theology. Theologians cite it, distinguish its doctrinal core from its contingent discipline, and apply it whenever a fresh dispute threatens to reopen a closed question. In that way the magisterium is not only an event in the past but a living memory in the present.",
            "For students of the magisterium, Conciliarism remains a working example of how the Church teaches: not by erasing history, but by judging history against the rule of faith she has always carried. The rejected positions remain valuable as warnings: they show how the language of Scripture and of ascetic zeal can be retained while the substance of the faith is denied. The intervention remains a model of how the Church decides under pressure. The result remains a fact of communion or of separation that later ages must still interpret. To read Conciliarism carefully is therefore to practice a Catholic habit of mind: to ask what was defined, what was only disciplined, what was received, and what still obliges. It is also to see that doctrine develops in clarity without dissolving into contradiction — that the Church can speak more explicitly in a later century without disowning what she confessed in an earlier one. That is why this controversy, and not only the abstract theory of infallibility, belongs on any serious map of the magisterium.",
            "In the end, Conciliarism teaches the same lesson every major controversy teaches when it is read from within the Catholic claim: the faith is public, the Church is visible, and her judgments are meant to be received. Private certainty, imperial convenience, and the pure remnant that separates from the apostolic see have all, at different times, offered themselves as safer paths. The Catholic path remains the harder one of communion under a judgment that can be named, dated, and handed on. Those who study the question, the positions, the intervention, and the act together are not merely learning a chapter of religious history. They are learning how the Church knows what she knows — and how she refuses to forget it."
